\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces This figure shows an example reconnection configuration on the scale of the ion skin depth for collisionless reconnection. The green arrows show the direction of flow into the x-point. Electrons trapped in a flux tube far from the x-point (with distribution function in panel A) are convected towards a region of lower field causing the flux tube to widen vertically (panel B). To enforce quasineutrality this requires the tubes to shorten in the parallel direction. The passing electrons (in green and purple which originate from a Maxwellian distribution) move to higher $v_\parallel $ whereas trapped electrons get smeared out causing the distribution to have an increasingly large $T_\parallel $ and smaller $T_\perp $.}}{6}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces The left panel shows an example reconnection region with a single field line in the outflow identified in red. The guide field is in the $\hat {\phi }$ direction. For low guide field (top right), the field line has a very small radius of curvature, $R_B$, since the field line is sharply bent and thus $\rho _e \gg R_B$. In this case the electron does not conserve magnetic moment and the distribution function is isotropized. In the case of high guide field (bottom right), the electron gyroradius is small relative to the radius of curvature and the pressure anisotropy is conserved.}}{8}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Simulated kinetic reconnection with changing guide field reproduced from \cite {Le_Egedal_Ohia_Daughton_Karimabadi_Lukin_2013}. The top four panels, (a - d), show results from a full mass ratio PIC simulation of reconnection with varying guide field ratios given by pink squares on left. At the bottom is a fluid simulation with a closure that allows for pressure anisotropy. As guide field changes, the outflow characteristics evolve from current that follows the separatrices at low field, an additional current jet that pierce the center of the outflow at moderate field, and a return to two jets at high field.}}{9}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces PIC simulations of reconnection from \cite {Le_Egedal_Ohia_Daughton_Karimabadi_Lukin_2013}. The top row shows a moderate guide field run and the bottom row a low guide field run. With sufficiently high field, the pressure anisotropy in panel (a) stays in the outflow as compared to the low guide field. This leads to a marginal firehose condition of order $0.5$ (panel (b)) in the outflow which supports sharply bent field lines and a current jet in the center of the outflow (panel (c)).}}{10}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces A CAD diagram of the Big Red Ball. The experiment starts with a background field generated by a Helmholtz coil (1) into which plasma is injected via a set of plasma guns (2). After some time, the plasma has equilibrated and then a reverse field is driven via either a set of individual coils or a drive cylinder (3). A reconnecting current layer travels radially inward from high radius to low, passing probes (4) along the way giving us the ability to trace the plasma parameters from the low radius inflow, into a reconnection outflow, and through the high radius inflow.}}{13}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Timeline of a single shot for TREX. The permanent magnets always generate a 0.4 T magnetic field at their surfaces that decays quickly into a minimal background field in the bulk of the machine. About four seconds before the start of the shot, the Helmholtz coil is turned on to allow the field to penetrate the three inch thick aluminum wall. Plasma is injected at 12 ms along with an optional toroidal field coil. The drive coils are then pulsed with a ringing current. The first quarter period, which will be analyzed throughout this thesis, is where a pressure wave travels radially inwards, followed by a shock, and finally a reconnection layer.}}{14}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.7}{\ignorespaces Measurements from two flux arrays for a single shot. Each flux array measures $\partial _t B_z$ at many $r$ and $z$ locations in a single $\phi $ plane. Each column is a different time in the experiment and each row shows a different parameter. The black line is the location where $B_z = 0$ and the grey lines are example magnetic fields. The top left plot shows the reconnection layer around $B_z = 0$ with $\partial _t B_z > 0$ at low radii caused by a shock. The top middle and right plots show the layer moving inwards. $B_z$ can be seen to change signs across the x-point, starting at a positive field set by the Helmholtz coil and ending with the reversed field from the drive coils. The bottom three plots show the out of plane current density which is peaked at $B_z = 0$ which is the reconnection layer.}}{15}{figure.caption.33}%
\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Experimental measurements from a single shot where B-dot probes are located along a radial chord at $z=0$. The drive cylinder, at $r = 0.6$ m, starts to reverse the field at $t = 0$. This releases the magnetic pressure at high radii allowing the plasma to expand which leads to a pressure wavefront moving radially inwards (orange). Later, a shock (green) is formed in front of the reconnection layer (purple dashed) which moves radially inwards. Once the pressure wavefront passes, the magnetic field lines start moving outwards following the flux contours (black). The passing of the wavefront leads to a reduction in the initial Helmholtz field before the shock or reconnection layer arrive.}}{18}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Pressure Wave Observations in B-dot Measurements}}{19}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Pressure Wave Observations in Simulations}}{21}{figure.caption.41}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces The simulated wavefront from a 1D linearized MHD simulation. Panel a shows the initial density profile and panel b shows the Alfv\'en speed (red) and fast magnetosonic speed (orange dashed) which are nearly the same. Panel c is the boundary velocity set by the applied loop voltage. Panel d is the velocity of the plasma which only begins to move outward once the wavefront passes with the different speed streamlines drawn on top. Panel e shows the expected measurements from a B-dot coil and flux contours which are similar to \ref {fig:experimental-evolution}, a.}}{31}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Experimental measurements of the normalized $\partial _t B_z$ measurements along a radial chord at $z = 18$ cm (the $z$-center of the drive coils is $z = 15$ cm). The radius of each the probe is given by the horizontal pale orange line. Each probe's measurements are normalized and offset to the probes radial location. The orange scatter points are the raw measurements and the blue line that intercepts the scatter points is an interpolation. The blue line has many oscillations that are due to noise in the system. The green smooth line is interpolated data after smoothing using a Savitzky-Golay filter.}}{33}{figure.caption.60}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces A CAD rendering of the BRB with the drive cylinder. The background $z$ field is set by the Helmholtz coil (A) and plasma is injected from plasma guns near $r = 0$ (B). After the plasma has equilibrated in the machine, a reversed field is driven by the drive cylinder (C) and the evolution of the magnetic field is done by arrays of B-dot probes on radial chords or at a single location per shot by a multi-tip Langmuir probe (D).}}{36}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.7}{\ignorespaces Plot of the initial plasma density measured by the Langmuir probe and pressure wave. The solid orange lines are measurements from different shots with filled area around each line setting the standard deviation. It can be seen that one shot can be easily identified as an outlier with exceptionally low density at low radii. The orange dashed line sets the minimum measurable density for the pressure wave. The green scatter points are measurements from the Langmuir probe with minimum density described in \ref {sss:multitip_min_density}.}}{37}{figure.caption.67}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.8}{\ignorespaces The left plot shows the expected magnetic field evolution from the simulation and the right plot shows the evolution of $\partial _t B_z$ in time for a variety of radii which are in positions similar to figure \ref {fig:pressure_wave_raw_bdot_traces}. The orange line shows the wavefront with orange dots being the points where the wave crosses the radial position. The right panel shows the growth of high frequency modes in the wavefront.}}{39}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.9}{\ignorespaces The phase and group velocities for the fast magnetosonic wave as a function of frequency. The MHD cutoff is set to when the phase velocity differs by more than 10\% of the fast magnetosonic speed derived from MHD. The plasma parameters here are $n = 10^{18}$ m$^{-3}$, $B = 5$ mT, $T_e = 5$ eV, $T_i = 1$ eV, and propagation angle $\theta = 90^\circ $.}}{40}{figure.caption.70}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.10}{\ignorespaces Example of DTW}}{42}{figure.caption.75}%
